WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.390 --> 00:00:03.830 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  we are 20 months from the 2020 election  what have you heard from the media about  the candidates maybe you've heard about  how likable they are or the odds that  can win you probably haven't heard much  about their plans to balance the budget  this far out from the election why are  we even talking about polls why aren't  we talking about policy on May 26 2016 I  was working on a presidential campaign  in our headquarters office I was sitting  at my desk working on briefing memos the  staff was excited that day because the 2  o'clock hour candidate was scheduled to  give a big policy speech about workers  rights in Las Vegas so we all wanted to  watch on TV as two o'clock approached a  group of the staff peeled away from  their work and huddled around the office  TVs which were set to all the major  networks the clock ticked down 158 159  but then two o'clock came and went and  none of the network's showed that policy  speech instead all of them showed that  that's a live feed of an empty podium at  another candidates rally in North Dakota  waiting for that candidate to take the  stage that candidate was so great for  ratings that even that image drew people  to the TV it was in that moment I  thought there's a crisis with political  media in this country it's not a  Republican Democrat crisis it's an  American problem it's a problem of the  trivial in our political media where  personality scandal and polling dominate  the coverage because that's what  generates ratings and profit I thought  in that moment it's time to rethink the  model of corporate political media think  back to the 2016 election what's the  first news story that comes to mind for  you about either candidate raise your  hand if that story was about policy like  economic or a foreign policy now  and if that story was about personality  scandal or polling like emails or tweets  exactly in the 2016 election according  to Harvard Kennedy School 10% of the  coverage was about policy two-thirds was  about personality scandal and polling  the coverage of our elections has become  a reality show and as a viewer I'm part  of the problem we all are it's hard to  turn off the TV or not click on the  headline when it's sensationalized but  imagine for a moment if our political  media were free from profit pressures  free to focus only on the stories they  think the public really needs to know  and free to pursue those stories with  death and with context over the last few  weeks I've been looking at headlines  about the 2020 candidates and some of  the leading news sites here's a sampling  of some of those headlines qumola  Harris's father  criticizes her from marijuana joke  Elizabeth Warren drinks a beer on  Instagram live and gets mixed reactions  grass roots not grass fed cory booker  could be our first vegan president in  the pale of winter trump's tan remains a  state secret  what about TV if you turn on cable news  in primetime what do you see  it's probably one of two things it could  be an opinion show where you have a  partisan host delivering a cross between  an op-ed and a newscast or it could be a  new show where you probably have a split  screen with a pundit on the left and one  on the right talking past each other  about the hot new controversy of the day  at the bottom of the screen in capital  letters it probably says breaking news  it's the same breaking news headline  whether there's been a natural disaster  or if a politician made a minor speaking  death so why should you care about this  and why now because reliable information  is an anchor of a functioning democracy  and we're drifting further and further  from that anchor let's think about the  situation we're in Americans are  increasingly uninformed three-quarters  can't name the three branches of  government on top of that you've all  heard about fake news there's a blurring  of the line between truth and fiction in  our news ecosystem an MIT study this  year found that false news stories are  70% more likely to get retweeted than  real news stories there's even a merging  AI that can mass produce fake news  making it that much harder to detect and  mitigate and finally less than half of  Americans trust the mass media that's  down from 70 percent in the 1970s in  this context what will happen in the  next national security emergency  how will we all agree on what's true we  take for granted that our political  media should be run that it's ideal for  it to be run as subsidiaries of  corporate giants but that system is  largely failing at what its core mission  should be to inform and educate what if  we considered our political media to be  a public good set aside as something  different from just another product in  our economy quality information is  central for people to make informed  decisions it has benefits to our whole  society there are already news  nonprofits doing great work like  ProPublica and NPR and you know what the  most trusted institution in the entire  country is PBS we could take that model  and build a new generation of political  media nonprofits with a strictly  nonpartisan educational mission and with  stable long term capital backing from  foundations and small donors one day  maybe you'll run a company that owns  media assets maybe you'll be looking to  invest for impact when you're in that  position consider that media is part of  the lifeblood of our democracy if you  can be a force for hiring journalists  with a not not-for-profit nonpartisan  mission when newsrooms are getting  decimated and pushed towards the trivial  that's having impact and if you work at  a social media company you can help  partner with quality news organizations  rather than sidelining and replacing  them these are all long-term ideas how  can you actually help today it's not  realistic to resolve that will stop  consuming infotainment altogether but  when we do consume it we could remind  ourselves that it's entertainment not a  replacement for real news the Washington  Post columnist Margaret Sullavan has  said that in an election the media  treats policy like a helping of steamed  broccoli shove to the side of the plate  broccoli gets a bad rap but it's healthy  and it's really important for a balanced  diet maybe we can make a small pledge  here today for every few stories we  consume between now and 2020 about the  political horse race we could try to  find and read and maybe even share a  story about policy in the strongest  democracy in the world we can build a  new landscape of political media a media  can be proud of that rises above the  fray to deliver substance and context  you can help make that a reality thank  you  [Applause]  [Music]

